  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe first two rounds of convection have departed the area. A\ncold front extends from southeast Lower MI into northern Indiana\nand will sweep southeast across the area this evening. Organized\nconvection is ongoing from the Thumb of Michigan into southern\nOntario, with a few more isolated cells farther southwest along\nthe front. The thunderstorms occurring from MI into ON are being\naided by a vort max that's very evident on water vapor imagery. \n\nThe thinking is that isolated to scattered convection will play\nout this evening across our area. The greatest confidence,\ncoverage, and potential severity is expected from far Northeast\nOH into Northwest PA, as the more organized activity aided by\nthe vort max from MI into ON builds east-southeast. Very dry\nmid-levels, a warm layer around 600mb, and weaker forcing make \ncoverage and severity of any convection farther west-southwest \nacross our area more questionable. MLCAPE of 1000-2000 J/KG,\nDCAPE values increasing to 700-1000 J/KG (higher west), and\neffective bulk shear values remaining in the 40-50kt range can\nsupport organized convection with a severe threat through this\nevening, especially across far Northeast OH and Northwest PA. \nPotential limitations include the generally poor mid-levels \nmentioned above (especially farther west), more modest low-level\nlapse rates farther east across our area due to limited \nheating, and effective storm relative helicity (SRH) values \ngradually weakening to 100-150 m2/s2. \n\nOverall, there's greater confidence in convection with \npotential for a couple or few severe storms across far Northeast\nOH and Northwest PA after 4 PM. Damaging winds would be the \nmain hazard, though a bit of hail and perhaps a low-end tornado \nthreat can't be fully ruled out here. Farther west, closer to \nand west of the I-77 corridor, more isolated storms are favored \nas the front moves through this evening with lower confidence in\nany severe weather threat. The environment can support a \ndamaging wind risk if we see any storms that are deep/organized \nenough to become severe farther west, though overall confidence \nis lower. Any severe risk should diminish by 9 PM, though a few \nshowers/storms may linger through about midnight as the front \ncontinues across the area and as we potentially see a lake \nenhanced shower or storm come off the lake behind the front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nQuite the airmass change behind the front as we head into\nWednesday. 850mb temperatures will dip to around 8C on\nWednesday, supporting surface highs struggling to get much above\n70. Overnight lows will dip into the 50s and lower 60s tonight,\nand then well into the 50s Wednesday and Thursday nights.\nTypical colder spots from interior eastern OH into interior PA \nshould see some 40s one or both of those nights. \n\nIt will be chilly enough aloft for some lake effect clouds and \nrain showers to come off the lake in the north-northwest flow \novernight tonight through Wednesday. This is not a particularly\nnoteworthy lake effect setup, just enough for meteorologists and\nenthusiasts to look at the satellite and radar late tonight or \nWednesday and remark \"oh, there's some lake effect on July \n22nd\". Lake effect should largely wind down by Wednesday night,\nthough guidance suggests that a land breeze may focus some lake\neffect near the south shore of Lake Erie through early Thursday,\nso the forecast maintains some low POPs through early Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nTroughing will gradually lift out of the Northeast through this\nweekend, allowing our temperatures to gradually warm from the\nend of this week into early next week. Temperatures may climb\nback to above normal by Sunday. There is some disagreement over\nwhen the next system comes in, it's possible Monday is quite \nwarm before the next cold front moves through. Mainly dry \nweather is expected through the weekend, with more organized \nshower and thunderstorm potential returning Monday into Tuesday \nas the next shortwave and cold front drop in.",
